# Redirects from the old site to this one (Cloudflare Workers static assets).
#
# Format: one rule per line, "from to status".
#   /old-path /new-path 301
# The status is optional and defaults to 302. Use 301 (permanent) for moved pages, which is
# what search engines expect. 302, 303, 307 and 308 also work.
# A "to" can be a full URL (https://example.com/page) for a move to another site.
# A line that starts with # is a comment and is ignored.
#
# Patterns: "*" matches the rest of a path and is reused as :splat, and :name matches one
# path segment and is reused by name.
#   /blog/* /insights/:splat 301
#   /services/:slug /what-i-do/:slug 301
# List static rules (no * and no :name) before dynamic ones. Wrangler counts a static-looking
# rule that comes after a dynamic one as dynamic.
# Duplicate "from" paths are ignored (the first one wins).
#
# Limits (wrangler 4, workers-shared parseRedirects): 2,000 static rules, 100 dynamic rules,
# 2,000 characters per line. Invalid lines are skipped with a warning at deploy time.
#
# Redirect rules are checked before static assets, so a rule wins over a page at the same path.
# Next.js copies everything in public/ into out/, so this file is built to out/_redirects and
# deployed with the site. Cloudflare does not serve _redirects or _headers as public files.
#
# Nothing here is a rule yet. The owner adds the old-site URLs at launch, then runs
#   node scripts/check-redirects.mjs old-urls.txt https://<site>
# against a preview URL and again against production.
#
# Check https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/redirects/ before launch
